module continuous_cost_min

// PRD v0_6 continuous-cost-minimisation §8.2 headline: Money-objective robustness
// floor wired end-to-end.  Task β (#4790) integration-gate example.
//
// A structural bracket must have a minimum wall thickness (stress/clearance
// requirement: thickness ≥ 2mm); more material means higher cost.  A pure
// cost-minimiser would park `thickness` EXACTLY on the 2mm boundary — fragile,
// since the strict `>` is unsatisfiable at equality.  α's robustness floor
// (task #4789) synthesises the auxiliary constraint
//
//   slack(thickness > 2mm) = thickness − 2mm ≥ m   (m ≈ 0.02 × 2mm = 0.04mm)
//
// which holds the resolved value strictly OFF the 2mm boundary and causes
// `reify eval` to emit one `RobustnessFloorApplied` (Info) diagnostic naming
// `cost_robustness_tradeoff(cost_expr, λ)` as the override.
//
// Integration test:
//   crates/reify-eval/tests/continuous_cost_min_example_e2e.rs
//   asserts 2mm < thickness < 2.5mm (measured: 2.040mm, the floored bound) AND
//   exactly one RobustnessFloorApplied Info containing 'cost_robustness_tradeoff'.
//
// # Why auto(free), not strict auto
//
// A strict `auto` triggers the uniqueness re-solve (perturbing the start and
// checking convergence within UNIQUENESS_SD_TOLERANCE).  Task #5711 moved that
// perturbation anchor onto the #5618 CONSTRAINT-DERIVED seed box; before it, the
// anchor came off the un-derived `effective_bounds` box and sat at ~9m for this
// mm-scale part.
//
// The ORIGINAL reason for `auto(free)` no longer holds: it was that the eval-layer
// solver returned the initially-feasible SEED (~10mm) via the `initially_feasible`
// fallback rather than a unique convergent point, so the perturbed re-solve did not
// reconverge and `ConstraintNonUnique` would mask the floor signal.  Since task #5618
// this example converges to 2.040mm — the floored bound, an actual argmin.
//
// It stays `auto(free)` anyway, deliberately, and task #5711 — which has now shipped
// and owned that question — RULED that it stays: #5711 resolved the uniqueness
// CONTRACT (§11.6's two disjunctive well-determinedness tests), not the β Money-floor
// path this file exercises, so flipping this example to a strict auto would be a
// behaviour change to an e2e-asserted example
// (crates/reify-eval/tests/continuous_cost_min_example_e2e.rs) that buys no coverage
// of what #5711 changed.  Whether a strict auto would now SURVIVE the uniqueness
// re-solve is still not re-tested here, and is still not a safe assumption.  Same
// reasoning as crates/reify-eval/tests/fixtures/cost_min_robustness_floor.ri.
//
// # Why Length, not bare Real
//
// This used to be a workaround.  `default_bounds_for` returns Length = [1µm, 10m] but
// dimensionless/Real = [−1e6, 1e6], and since `AutoParam.bounds` was always `None` in
// production that default box was the ONLY box the solver had.  A cost-minimiser
// driving toward the lower bound would push a Real param toward −1e6 (unphysical; the
// slack/penalty interplay can yield infeasible/undef), so choosing Length bought a
// physically-bounded fallback.  Recorded then as "PRD §9 Q2 tactical confirmation —
// no fix required".
//
// Task #5618 removed the need for it.  The solver now derives the seed and clamp
// boxes from the inequality constraints themselves, so `thickness > 2mm` brackets the
// param regardless of its dimension — a bare `Real` here would be bracketed by the
// same constraint rather than by `default_bounds_for`, and would no longer run away
// to −1e6.  (#5618's headline regression is precisely a dimensionless auto bracketed
// away from 0, which used to report a false RobustnessFloorInfeasible.)
//
// The model deliberately KEEPS Length: a wall thickness is a length, and the type
// carries that meaning to every reader and every unit check.  What changed is that it
// is now a modelling choice rather than a solver workaround.
structure CostMinBracket {
    param thickness : Length = auto(free)
    param unit_cost : Money = 5USD

    // Stress/clearance minimum-thickness: the wall must be ≥ 2mm to carry the
    // load / hold clearance.  Pure cost-min would park thickness exactly on this
    // boundary (zero margin → fragile); α's robustness floor holds it off.
    constraint thickness > 2mm

    // Closed-form Money material cost: more material (thicker wall) costs more,
    // so cost-min drives thickness toward the 2mm boundary.  Money × dimensionless
    // = Money ⇒ objective_is_money ⇒ robustness floor synthesised.
    minimize unit_cost * (thickness / 1mm)
}
